\paragraph{Text encoder as baseline.}
Text probes use a single 384-dimensional encoder (all-MiniLM-L6-v2). Larger encoders or domain-fine-tuned models might narrow the observed HS-text accuracy gap at 7--8B scale. Our comparison tests hidden-state probes against one specific text representation, not against the best achievable text baseline.

\paragraph{Correlation, not causation.}

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Text encoder as baseline.}
The primary text baseline uses all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). A robustness check with BGE-large (1024d) confirms directional HS advantage on all four models but with narrowed effect sizes; only OT-7B remains significant after Holm-Bonferroni correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Domain-fine-tuned encoders might further narrow the gap.

\paragraph{Bootstrap with small samples.}
Bootstrap confidence intervals at $n{=}19$--$20$ (32B models) may exhibit below-nominal coverage~\citep{efron1993bootstrap}. The minimum $p$-value resolution with 1000 resamples is $10^{-3}$, and reported $p{<}0.001$ values should be interpreted as upper bounds.

\paragraph{Correlation, not causation.}

